1 NOTE "In 1842, Pleasant Miles Powell inherited Powellton from his father: he i
2 CONC n turn left same to his daughter Mary Jane ( the second of daughters so n
2 CONC amed). Mary Jane, who never married, left Powellton to her sister, J. E
2 CONC lizabeth Brookshire. The big house with 300 acres was purchased in 1939 b
2 CONC y Palmer Nicholson, County Road Commissioner: he restored the house. Mi
2 CONC ss Sadie Stanback, a granddaughter of Col. Pleasant Miles Powell sold 1
2 CONC 100 acres of this property in 1963 to the Reigal Paper Company.
2 CONT In 1858, Pleasant Miles Powell was elected by the Legislature as a memb
2 CONC er of Gov. Ellis' staff, whereby he received the honorary title of Colo
2 CONC nel. He died at the age of 55 of Dropsy. This man was buried in an upri
2 CONC ght position: the coffin was built around the chair he was sitting in w
2 CONC hen he died. His tombstone was placed over his head and it stands out a
2 CONC bout three feet from the others in the row"
2 CONT
2 CONT The above information was give to me in his letter of 12/30/2000 by Pre
2 CONC ston Johnson, husband of Leslie Hall who is my 1st cousin.
2 CONT William Hill Powell, Jr.,!/08/2001, Huntsville,TX
