1 CHAN
2 DATE 7 JAN 2026
1 NOTE In 1775, Charles Robinson of Anson Co., a member of the General Assembl
2 CONC y of North Carolina, asked the governor for miltary assistance in quell
2 CONC ing an Indian uprising on the southwestern frontier and Benjamin Powell w
2 CONC as one of the soldiers sent there (See War Records, Dept of Archives, R
2 CONC aleigh, NC). Due to the numerious Indian raids, the soldiers were kept i
2 CONC n Anson Co. for many months at which time Benjamin Powell made the acqa
2 CONC intance of Charles Robinson's daughter, Elizabeth.
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT Benjamin was a private in the Rev. War, North Carolina Line, Capt. McNe
2 CONC es Reg. (N.C.Army Accts., Vol. 6, p. 12 folio4, also Vol. 9, p.83, N. C
2 CONC . Book of Settlements No. 30, p. 93 and No. 78, p.78.)
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT His tombstone reads "Died 1808, aged 53". The Brookshire Cemetery was v
2 CONC isited by Mary Hall Burwell (my 1st cousin) and her husband Ven Burwell i
2 CONC n the November, 2000. Her directions for locating the cemetary are as f
2 CONC ollows, "Brookshire Cemetery, Richmond Co., NC. Take Hwy 73 from Ellerb
2 CONC ee in Richmond C0. about 9-10 miles west. Stop at Hardister's Old Store (
2 CONC not marked) and turn left on farm road about 200 yds. south. Cemetery i
2 CONC s between road and a vineyard on Summer Duck  Farm. Farm orginally owne
2 CONC d by W. H. Parker but new owners are from Charlotte."  Mary took some p
2 CONC ictures of the cemetery which is heavily over grown by large trees but i
2 CONC t appears that most of the tombstones are standing.
2 CONT
2 CONT This information was forwarded to me by Preston Johnson, husband of Les
2 CONC lie Hall (my 1st cousin) along with information on Benjamin Powell and 4 g
2 CONC enerations of descendants.
2 CONT
2 CONT Entered by William Hill Powell, Jr. January 7, 2001
